I included neither operations request, because the node's `with` clause doesn't grant the authority they need:

1. **Log file `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`:** the node has no filesystem capability, and the component has no WASI filesystem import.
2. **`https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=...`:** `HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>` only allows `feeds.example.com`. A request to another host would trap at the boundary. It would also send the input's text to a third party.

If operations wants either, the graph needs a new capability, such as a logger or a wider HTTP allowlist.

On `ensures notified == true`: if the notifier returns `false`, the node traps instead of emitting a report with `notified: false`. That way every output it produces satisfies the contract.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat at {}s: fetched {} chars", seconds, fetched);
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);
        if !notified {
            // Contract: every produced output has notified == true.
            panic!("notification was not accepted");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

